+# What Baltor is
+
+Kind: operating guide for a paying customer. It is the first page to read. It
+says what the hosted service holds, what stays on your machine, how the
+library is sorted and where to go next.
+
+Baltor is a hosted library of reviewed material for the steps of a task. Your
+coding tool searches it, reads a short reference, and downloads only the item
+that a step needs. You keep your own tools and your own model access.
+
+Baltor is the public name. Loop Engine is the name of the repository, the
+Python package and the `loop-engine` command. These pages use the exact
+technical names wherever you copy one into a file or read one in an answer.
+
+## Where each part runs
+
+```text
+Where each part runs
+├── Baltor, at https://app.baltor.ai
+│   ├── Holds reviewed material and who may see each item
+│   ├── Answers a search with short references, never a body
+│   ├── Delivers the body of one item you chose, with its digest
+│   └── Records each download, so you can read your own usage
+└── Your machine
+    ├── Your coding tool, such as Codex, OpenCode or Claude Code
+    ├── Your model access and your model keys
+    ├── Your project files, which the service never asks for
+    └── What you do with the material you downloaded
+```
+
+The service receives the search text you send, the identity of the item you
+ask for, and your credential. It never receives your repository, your prompts
+or your model keys.
+
+## Baltor never runs a model
+
+The service makes no model call. You bring your own model access, for example
+Ollama on your own machine, Ollama Cloud, a model server on your own network,
+or a hosted provider. Model costs stay between you and your provider, and
+Baltor never asks for a model key.
+
+## How the library is sorted
+
+Every item names its family, the place its body is kept, and its kind.
+
+| Field | Value on the deployed service | What it means |
+|---|---|---|
+| `family` | `harness` | Files a coding tool already reads from its working folder or its settings. |
+| `source_layer` | `harness_local` | Where the body of a harness item is kept. |
+| `kind` | `skill` | Every kind is one of `reusable_code`, `skill`, `tool` and `instruction_file`. |
+
+The service serves the harness family unless its host names more families.
+The library defines two more: `loop_native`, built for the engine itself, and
+`open_knowledge`, general knowledge in open formats. Their bodies sit in the
+four persistent intelligence layers: Context Intelligence, Code Intelligence,
+Runtime History and Solution Intelligence, and User Feedback Intelligence.
+
+Every item also carries a digest of its body, a licence, a size, a purpose and
+a statement of how it was approved. An item can be one file or a package of
+several files that a coding tool reads together.
+[Searching and retrieving](service-searching-and-retrieving.md) shows how to
+download each file of a package.
+
+## What is in the library today
+
+Checked on September 23, 2026, the deployed service registers 43 approved
+items. Each one is a skill in one Markdown file. Three reviewers who did not
+write an item approved it before it was registered, and the library grows
+only through the same review.
+
+Your account sees only the items it holds a grant for, and a request can
+narrow that set. Nine of the 43 items declare an effect, such as reading
+files or running a command. A request that does not name that effect is not
+offered those items, so an account granted all 43 is offered 34 by default.
+
+## Four things to know
+
+| Fact | What it means |
+|---|---|
+| A digest is not a review. | Every answer names its `qualification_basis`. `host_attested` means the host attests that the item was approved before it was registered. The service does not review the item again when it answers, and a manifest says `verify_before_use`. |
+| A reference is not a body. | A search answer states `bodies_loaded` as false. You choose, then you download, and your permission is checked again at the download. |
+| A download is not a use. | The service records that you downloaded an item. Whether your tool used it well is your own record. |
+| A refusal is a record. | Every refusal is the same record, `service_http_error/v1`, with a stable code you can act on. |
+
+## What Baltor does not do
+
+- It does not run code, open files or reach into your machine.
+- It does not call a model, hold your model keys or spend money on models.
+- It does not approve material because you downloaded it, ran it or liked it.
+  Approval is a separate, independent review.
+
+## Where to go next
+
+| You want to | Read |
+|---|---|
+| Ask for an account | [Get started](https://app.baltor.ai/get-started) |
+| Connect your coding tool and make a first search | [Get set up](service-getting-set-up.md) |
+| Know what an account gives you | [Your account](service-your-account.md) |
+| Search, choose an item and download it | [Searching and retrieving](service-searching-and-retrieving.md) |
+| Know what is free and what is measured | [Usage and what you pay for](service-usage-and-what-you-pay-for.md) |
+| Understand a refusal you just met | [Troubleshooting](service-troubleshooting.md) |
+| Write your own client | [Serving and connections](service-serving-and-connections.md) |
+
+To run the engine on your own machine instead of using the hosted service,
+read [the quick start](quick-start.md).
