+# Your account
+
+Kind: operating guide for a paying customer. It explains how to get an
+account, the four checks behind every download, the two kinds of credential,
+and the plan.
+
+An account gives your coding tools the reviewed library: search, references,
+downloads and a record of your usage. Read this page before you set up, so the
+words on the account page make sense.
+
+## How to get an account
+
+Accounts are created by invitation until registration opens. A new customer
+starts at [Get started](https://app.baltor.ai/get-started), which takes your
+request for an invitation.
+
+1. You leave your email address and, if you like, a short note.
+2. An operator reads each request and decides.
+3. An invited person receives an email with a link to set a password. The
+   link works once. The email also carries a discount code for checkout.
+4. You sign in at `/login`. Your account page is `/account`.
+
+An invited account pays nothing while invitations go out in small groups. It
+holds access through an operator grant, not a payment.
+
+You can see that registration is closed without signing in:
+
+```bash
+curl -sS https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/capabilities
+```
+
+In the `website` section of the answer, `registration_available` is false and
+`access_profile` is `operator_provisioned`. `waitlist_available` is true while
+the service takes invitation requests.
+
+## One account is one tenant
+
+Your account is a tenant. The service decides every request about that tenant
+and resolves it again on every request, because sessions are stateless.
+
+Read your own account at any time. This is free:
+
+```bash
+curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/session
+```
+
+The answer is a `service_session/v1` record that holds a
+`service_principal/v1`. Five fields in it decide almost everything:
+
+| Field | What it decides |
+|---|---|
+| `tenant_id` | Which account this is. Material is granted to an account, not to a person or a machine. |
+| `namespace` | Where this account's own records are kept. |
+| `key_id` | Which credential you used. It is an identifier, never the secret. |
+| `entitlement` | Whether you may download bodies at all. |
+| `scopes` | Which operations this credential may perform. |
+
+## Four checks behind every download
+
+A download passes four separate checks, in this order. A refusal names the
+one that failed.
+
+```text
+Can I download this item?
+├── 1. Is the account enabled?                    no: unauthorized, 401
+├── 2. Does the credential hold provisioning:read? no: insufficient_scope, 403
+├── 3. Does the account hold a grant for it?       no: item_unavailable, 404
+└── 4. Is the account's entitlement bodies?       no: body_forbidden, 403
+```
+
+### The entitlement
+
+The entitlement belongs to the account. It has two values.
+
+| Value | What it allows |
+|---|---|
+| `metadata` | Search, list and manifest. You see what exists for you, with its digest, size, licence and purpose. You cannot download a body. |
+| `bodies` | Everything above, and downloading the body of an item you hold a grant for. |
+
+An entitlement comes from a subscription or from an operator grant. It never
+comes from a token, and a new token does not change it.
+
+### The scopes
+
+A scope belongs to the credential. The service defines five.
+
+| Scope | What it permits |
+|---|---|
+| `provisioning:metadata` | Search, discover, list and manifest. |
+| `provisioning:read` | Reading a body, at `/api/v1/download` or as an inline read. |
+| `usage:read` | Reading this account's own usage. |
+| `billing:manage` | Starting a checkout or opening the payment portal. |
+| `access:manage` | Operator administration: issuing and revoking tokens for accounts, and deciding on invitation requests. A customer does not need it. |
+
+A client token never carries more scopes than the account holds. Asking for
+more is refused with `scope_escalation_refused`. Two refusals name a missing
+scope. `insufficient_scope` means the token lacks it, and you can fix that by
+creating a new token. `scope_required` means the account lacks it, and only
+your operator can change that.
+
+### The grants
+
+A grant connects one account to one item. Without a grant, the item is not
+listed, not found by search, and a manifest for it answers `item_unavailable`
+with status 404. The answer is the same whether the item does not exist or
+exists for another account, so no account can map another account's library.
+
+Ask the service what you hold instead of guessing. The discover answer reports
+`items_held` and `bodies_available`:
+
+```bash
+curl -sS -X POST https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/provisioning \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"record_type": "service_provisioning_request/v1", "operation": "discover"}'
+```
+
+A request can also narrow what it is offered. An item that declares an effect
+your request does not name in `authority_effects`, such as running a command,
+is listed under `withheld` with its reason. A manifest or a read of it is
+refused with `item_withheld`. That is not a missing permission. Name the
+effect in the request when your step may have it.
+
+## Two kinds of credential
+
+The account page and your coding tool use different credentials, and the
+service keeps them apart.
+
+| Credential | Where you use it | What it can do |
+|---|---|---|
+| A browser session | `/account`, after you sign in | Create, list and revoke client tokens, and start a checkout. |
+| A client token | Your coding tool or a script | Search, read manifests, download bodies and read usage. |
+
+A client token cannot manage credentials. That attempt is refused with
+`browser_session_required`, status 403, so a leaked token cannot make more
+tokens.
+
+The account page reads the token policy from `/api/v1/account/access`, in the
+`service_client_access_options/v1` record:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `allowed_scopes` | The scopes a new token may carry. |
+| `maximum_active_tokens` | How many tokens you may hold at once. Past it, a new token is refused with `access_token_limit_reached`. |
+| `maximum_lifetime_seconds` | The longest lifetime you may ask for. Longer is refused with `access_lifetime_exceeded`. |
+| `default_lifetime_seconds` | The lifetime you get when you ask for none. |
+| `maximum_token_records` | How many token records the account keeps. When they are all used, a new token is refused with `access_token_history_limit_reached`. |
+
+Three facts about a token secret:
+
+- It is shown once. The service stores only a digest, so nobody, your
+  operator included, can show it to you again.
+- It expires. The account page shows a `state` beside each token, and a token
+  whose state is `expired` or `revoked` is refused on its next request.
+- Revoking takes effect on the next request. It does not recall material that
+  was already downloaded.
+
+[Get set up](service-getting-set-up.md) shows how to create a token and where
+to keep it.
+
+## The plan
+
+One plan, Baltor Pro, at $29 a month. Search is free. One downloaded item is
+the measured unit, and there is no overage billing.
+[Usage and what you pay for](service-usage-and-what-you-pay-for.md) lists what
+is measured and what is not. A new customer starts at
+[Get started](https://app.baltor.ai/get-started).
+
+A signed-in account subscribes, or opens the payment portal, from its account
+page when checkout is available. The page reads what the service offers your
+account:
+
+```bash
+curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/billing/plans
+```
+
+The answer is a `billing_session_options/v1` record. When
+`checkout_available` or `portal_available` is false, `unavailable_reason` says
+why. `billing_sessions_not_installed` means the host has not switched payments
+on. `billing_scope_required` means the credential does not hold
+`billing:manage`. The other reasons describe the host, and only your operator
+can change them. The portal opens only after your first checkout, which
+creates your payment customer.
+
+## What an account does not give you
+
+- It does not give you a model or pay for model calls. Baltor never runs a
+  model. You bring your own model access, for example Ollama on your own
+  machine, Ollama Cloud or a model server on your own network.
+- It does not let the service run anything on your machine.
+- It does not make downloaded material independently qualified. Every answer
+  names its `qualification_basis`, and `host_attested` means that the host
+  attests the approval.
+- It does not give each token its own tenant. The token policy says so
+  directly: tokens share the selected tenant's grants and usage.
+
