+++ b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/docs/usage-and-what-you-pay-for.html
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+<p><a href="/docs/searching-and-retrieving">Searching and retrieving</a> shows the requests and answers that produce a measured unit. This page is the shorter answer to the question people ask first, which is what this will cost.</p>
+<h2 id="one-measured-unit-is-one-downloaded-item">One measured unit is one downloaded item</h2>
+<pre><code class="language-text">What the service measures
+├── Measured
+│   └── a body read the service accepted and recorded, one unit, named provisioned_item
+└── Never measured
+    ├── searching, at /api/v1/retrieval or through the intelligence_search tool
+    ├── discovering and listing what this account may see
+    ├── a manifest, including its digest, size and licence
+    ├── any refusal that happens before the body is released
+    ├── reading this account's own usage
+    └── a repeat of a body read that carries the same request_id</code></pre>
+<p>The service states this itself rather than leaving it to a document. The discover answer carries <code>never_metered</code>, and the four entries it lists are listing authorized items, a manifest with digests and sizes, a refusal before metering, and reading the tenant's own usage. It also carries <code>metered_unit</code>, which is <code>provisioned_item</code>.</p>
+<p>An item that is a package of several files is still one item. In a release that includes catalogue releases, every file of one package fetched with the same <code>request_id</code> is recorded once, as one unit. Release 16 and every earlier release do not read that request shape. <a href="/docs/searching-and-retrieving#one-file-of-a-package">Searching and retrieving</a> shows the request.</p>
+<h2 id="the-plan">The plan</h2>
+<p>One paid plan, named Baltor Pro, at 29.00 US dollars each month, paid in US dollars. There is no annual price, no seat price and no second tier at launch. Search is free. There is no overage billing at launch. An invited account is free, through an operator grant rather than a payment.</p>
+<h2 id="before-you-download-the-manifest-tells-you-whether-it-will-be-measured">Before you download, the manifest tells you whether it will be measured</h2>
+<p>A manifest is free, and it answers both of the questions that matter before a download. Ask for one, with the identity your search returned in place of <code>ITEM_IDENTITY</code>:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS -X POST https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/provisioning \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"record_type": "service_provisioning_request/v1",
+       "operation": "manifest", "identity": "ITEM_IDENTITY"}'</code></pre>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field in the answer</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>body_allowed</code></td><td>Whether this account, with this credential, may download the body at all.</td></tr><tr><td><code>metering_policy</code></td><td><code>required</code> when a successful download records one unit. <code>unmetered</code> when it does not.</td></tr><tr><td><code>size_bytes</code></td><td>How large the body is, before you fetch it.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>Those are the only two policy values. A <code>metering_policy</code> the service does not define is refused rather than guessed.</p>
+<h2 id="where-your-usage-is">Where your usage is</h2>
+<p>Reading your own usage is free and needs the <code>usage:read</code> scope.</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/usage</code></pre>
+<p>The answer is a <code>durable_tenant_usage/v1</code> record with exactly four fields beside its record type:</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tenant_id</code></td><td>The account these numbers belong to.</td></tr><tr><td><code>records</code></td><td>How many measured body reads are recorded for this account.</td></tr><tr><td><code>totals</code></td><td>The same count grouped by unit, so <code>{"provisioned_item": 10.0}</code> means ten downloaded items.</td></tr><tr><td><code>durability</code></td><td><code>durable</code> when the count is written to durable storage.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>This is the authoritative count. It is not an estimate and it is not delayed by a reporting window. Read it before you repeat anything that downloads a body.</p>
+<p>The same numbers appear on <code>/account</code> in the browser, for the account you are signed in as.</p>
+<h2 id="retrying-without-being-measured-twice">Retrying without being measured twice</h2>
+<p>Every body read carries a <code>request_id</code> that you choose. It identifies one download attempt.</p>
+<pre><code class="language-text">A download that failed with an uncertain outcome
+├── Repeat it with the SAME request_id
+│   ├── You get the same bytes
+│   └── No second unit is recorded
+└── Use a NEW request_id
+    └── This is a second measured read, on purpose</code></pre>
+<p>That rule is what makes the three uncertain refusals safe to handle:</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Status</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>meter_commit_unknown</code></td><td>503</td><td>The unit may or may not have been recorded.</td><td>Retry with the same <code>request_id</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>deadline_exceeded</code></td><td>504</td><td>The operation did not finish in time. Work may still be running.</td><td>Read <code>/api/v1/usage</code>, or retry with the same <code>request_id</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>commit_unknown</code></td><td>503</td><td>A durable write was attempted and its outcome is unknown.</td><td>Read the current state before acting.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>None of these means the work failed. Read first, act second.</p>
+<h2 id="when-a-download-is-accepted-the-answer-says-so">When a download is accepted, the answer says so</h2>
+<p>An inline read at <code>/api/v1/provisioning</code> returns the body inside a <code>provisioning_body/v2</code> record together with its metering acknowledgment, which is a <code>provisioning_meter_acknowledgment/v1</code> record. Read three fields from it:</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>metered</code></td><td>Whether this particular read recorded a unit.</td></tr><tr><td><code>metered_unit</code></td><td><code>provisioned_item</code>, or absent when nothing was recorded.</td></tr><tr><td><code>metering_acknowledgment</code></td><td>Carries <code>committed</code> and <code>durability</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p><code>durability</code> has three possible values, <code>volatile</code>, <code>durable</code> and <code>unknown</code>. <code>durable</code> means the record is written to durable storage. <code>unknown</code> means the service cannot state the outcome, which is the same situation as <code>meter_commit_unknown</code> and is handled the same way.</p>
+<h2 id="two-failures-that-are-about-the-meter-not-about-you">Two failures that are about the meter, not about you</h2>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>meter_unavailable</code></td><td>400</td><td>The item requires metering and the meter is not installed on this host.</td><td>Report it to your operator. Nothing you send changes it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>meter_commit_unknown</code></td><td>503</td><td>The unit may or may not have been recorded.</td><td>Retry with the same <code>request_id</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>A refusal before the body is released is never measured. That includes every credential, permission, request-shape, size and rate refusal in <a href="/docs/serving-and-connections">the complete refusal table</a>.</p>
+<h2 id="what-the-service-does-not-measure">What the service does not measure</h2>
+<ul><li>It does not measure the size of a body. One downloaded item is one unit whether it is 300 bytes or the largest body the host will deliver.</li><li>It does not measure a search, however many results it returns.</li><li>It does not measure model calls, because it does not make any. Your model spending is with your provider and never reaches this service.</li><li>It does not measure whether you used the material. A download is a download.</li></ul>
+<h2 id="related-pages">Related pages</h2>
+<ul><li><a href="/docs/searching-and-retrieving">Searching and retrieving</a></li><li><a href="/docs/your-account">Your account</a></li><li><a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/alisonjieli-png/loop-engine/blob/main/docs/guides/billing-setup-and-pricing.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Billing setup and pricing</a>, for the engineering record behind the price</li></ul>
diff --git a/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/docs/what-baltor-is.html b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/docs/what-baltor-is.html
