index 00000000..6ad3e602
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/docs/what-baltor-is.html
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+<p>Baltor is a hosted library of reviewed material for the steps of a task. Your coding tool searches it, reads a short reference, and downloads only the item that a step needs. You keep your own tools and your own model access.</p>
+<p>Baltor is the public name. Loop Engine is the name of the repository, the Python package and the <code>loop-engine</code> command. These pages use the exact technical names wherever you copy one into a file or read one in an answer.</p>
+<h2 id="where-each-part-runs">Where each part runs</h2>
+<pre><code class="language-text">Where each part runs
+├── Baltor, at https://app.baltor.ai
+│   ├── Holds reviewed material and who may see each item
+│   ├── Answers a search with short references, never a body
+│   ├── Delivers the body of one item you chose, with its digest
+│   └── Records each download, so you can read your own usage
+└── Your machine
+    ├── Your coding tool, such as Codex, OpenCode or Claude Code
+    ├── Your model access and your model keys
+    ├── Your project files, which the service never asks for
+    └── What you do with the material you downloaded</code></pre>
+<p>The service receives the search text you send, the identity of the item you ask for, and your credential. It never receives your repository, your prompts or your model keys.</p>
+<h2 id="baltor-never-runs-a-model">Baltor never runs a model</h2>
+<p>The service makes no model call. You bring your own model access, for example Ollama on your own machine, Ollama Cloud, a model server on your own network, or a hosted provider. Model costs stay between you and your provider, and Baltor never asks for a model key.</p>
+<h2 id="how-the-library-is-sorted">How the library is sorted</h2>
+<p>Every item names its family, the place its body is kept, and its kind.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value on the deployed service</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>family</code></td><td><code>harness</code></td><td>Files a coding tool already reads from its working folder or its settings.</td></tr><tr><td><code>source_layer</code></td><td><code>harness_local</code></td><td>Where the body of a harness item is kept.</td></tr><tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>skill</code></td><td>Every kind is one of <code>reusable_code</code>, <code>skill</code>, <code>tool</code> and <code>instruction_file</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>The service serves the harness family unless its host names more families. The library defines two more: <code>loop_native</code>, built for the engine itself, and <code>open_knowledge</code>, general knowledge in open formats. Their bodies sit in the four persistent intelligence layers: Context Intelligence, Code Intelligence, Runtime History and Solution Intelligence, and User Feedback Intelligence.</p>
+<p>Every item also carries a digest of its body, a licence, a size, a purpose and a statement of how it was approved. An item can be one file or a package of several files that a coding tool reads together. <a href="/docs/searching-and-retrieving">Searching and retrieving</a> shows how to download each file of a package.</p>
+<h2 id="what-is-in-the-library-today">What is in the library today</h2>
+<p>Checked on September 23, 2026, the deployed service registers 43 approved items. Each one is a skill in one Markdown file. Three reviewers who did not write an item approved it before it was registered, and the library grows only through the same review.</p>
+<p>Your account sees only the items it holds a grant for, and a request can narrow that set. Nine of the 43 items declare an effect, such as reading files or running a command. A request that does not name that effect is not offered those items, so an account granted all 43 is offered 34 by default.</p>
+<h2 id="four-things-to-know">Four things to know</h2>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A digest is not a review.</td><td>Every answer names its <code>qualification_basis</code>. <code>host_attested</code> means the host attests that the item was approved before it was registered. The service does not review the item again when it answers, and a manifest says <code>verify_before_use</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>A reference is not a body.</td><td>A search answer states <code>bodies_loaded</code> as false. You choose, then you download, and your permission is checked again at the download.</td></tr><tr><td>A download is not a use.</td><td>The service records that you downloaded an item. Whether your tool used it well is your own record.</td></tr><tr><td>A refusal is a record.</td><td>Every refusal is the same record, <code>service_http_error/v1</code>, with a stable code you can act on.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<h2 id="what-baltor-does-not-do">What Baltor does not do</h2>
+<ul><li>It does not run code, open files or reach into your machine.</li><li>It does not call a model, hold your model keys or spend money on models.</li><li>It does not approve material because you downloaded it, ran it or liked it. Approval is a separate, independent review.</li></ul>
+<h2 id="where-to-go-next">Where to go next</h2>
+<table><thead><tr><th>You want to</th><th>Read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ask for an account</td><td><a href="/get-started">Get started</a></td></tr><tr><td>Connect your coding tool and make a first search</td><td><a href="/setup">Get set up</a></td></tr><tr><td>Know what an account gives you</td><td><a href="/docs/your-account">Your account</a></td></tr><tr><td>Search, choose an item and download it</td><td><a href="/docs/searching-and-retrieving">Searching and retrieving</a></td></tr><tr><td>Know what is free and what is measured</td><td><a href="/docs/usage-and-what-you-pay-for">Usage and what you pay for</a></td></tr><tr><td>Understand a refusal you just met</td><td><a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></td></tr><tr><td>Write your own client</td><td><a href="/docs/serving-and-connections">Serving and connections</a></td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>To run the engine on your own machine instead of using the hosted service, read <a href="https://github.com/alisonjieli-png/loop-engine/blob/main/docs/guides/quick-start.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">the quick start</a>.</p>
