+++ b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/docs/your-account.html
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+<p>An account gives your coding tools the reviewed library: search, references, downloads and a record of your usage. Read this page before you set up, so the words on the account page make sense.</p>
+<h2 id="how-to-get-an-account">How to get an account</h2>
+<p>Accounts are created by invitation until registration opens. A new customer starts at <a href="/get-started">Get started</a>, which takes your request for an invitation.</p>
+<ol><li>You leave your email address and, if you like, a short note.</li><li>An operator reads each request and decides.</li><li>An invited person receives an email with a link to set a password. The link works once. The email also carries a discount code for checkout.</li><li>You sign in at <code>/login</code>. Your account page is <code>/account</code>.</li></ol>
+<p>An invited account pays nothing while invitations go out in small groups. It holds access through an operator grant, not a payment.</p>
+<p>You can see that registration is closed without signing in:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/capabilities</code></pre>
+<p>In the <code>website</code> section of the answer, <code>registration_available</code> is false and <code>access_profile</code> is <code>operator_provisioned</code>. <code>waitlist_available</code> is true while the service takes invitation requests.</p>
+<h2 id="one-account-is-one-tenant">One account is one tenant</h2>
+<p>Your account is a tenant. The service decides every request about that tenant and resolves it again on every request, because sessions are stateless.</p>
+<p>Read your own account at any time. This is free:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/session</code></pre>
+<p>The answer is a <code>service_session/v1</code> record that holds a <code>service_principal/v1</code>. Five fields in it decide almost everything:</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tenant_id</code></td><td>Which account this is. Material is granted to an account, not to a person or a machine.</td></tr><tr><td><code>namespace</code></td><td>Where this account's own records are kept.</td></tr><tr><td><code>key_id</code></td><td>Which credential you used. It is an identifier, never the secret.</td></tr><tr><td><code>entitlement</code></td><td>Whether you may download bodies at all.</td></tr><tr><td><code>scopes</code></td><td>Which operations this credential may perform.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<h2 id="four-checks-behind-every-download">Four checks behind every download</h2>
+<p>A download passes four separate checks, in this order. A refusal names the one that failed.</p>
+<pre><code class="language-text">Can I download this item?
+├── 1. Is the account enabled?                    no: unauthorized, 401
+├── 2. Does the credential hold provisioning:read? no: insufficient_scope, 403
+├── 3. Does the account hold a grant for it?       no: item_unavailable, 404
+└── 4. Is the account's entitlement bodies?       no: body_forbidden, 403</code></pre>
+<h3 id="the-entitlement">The entitlement</h3>
+<p>The entitlement belongs to the account. It has two values.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>What it allows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Search, list and manifest. You see what exists for you, with its digest, size, licence and purpose. You cannot download a body.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bodies</code></td><td>Everything above, and downloading the body of an item you hold a grant for.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>An entitlement comes from a subscription or from an operator grant. It never comes from a token, and a new token does not change it.</p>
+<h3 id="the-scopes">The scopes</h3>
+<p>A scope belongs to the credential. The service defines five.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it permits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>provisioning:metadata</code></td><td>Search, discover, list and manifest.</td></tr><tr><td><code>provisioning:read</code></td><td>Reading a body, at <code>/api/v1/download</code> or as an inline read.</td></tr><tr><td><code>usage:read</code></td><td>Reading this account's own usage.</td></tr><tr><td><code>billing:manage</code></td><td>Starting a checkout or opening the payment portal.</td></tr><tr><td><code>access:manage</code></td><td>Operator administration: issuing and revoking tokens for accounts, and deciding on invitation requests. A customer does not need it.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>A client token never carries more scopes than the account holds. Asking for more is refused with <code>scope_escalation_refused</code>. Two refusals name a missing scope. <code>insufficient_scope</code> means the token lacks it, and you can fix that by creating a new token. <code>scope_required</code> means the account lacks it, and only your operator can change that.</p>
+<h3 id="the-grants">The grants</h3>
+<p>A grant connects one account to one item. Without a grant, the item is not listed, not found by search, and a manifest for it answers <code>item_unavailable</code> with status 404. The answer is the same whether the item does not exist or exists for another account, so no account can map another account's library.</p>
+<p>Ask the service what you hold instead of guessing. The discover answer reports <code>items_held</code> and <code>bodies_available</code>:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS -X POST https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/provisioning \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"record_type": "service_provisioning_request/v1", "operation": "discover"}'</code></pre>
+<p>A request can also narrow what it is offered. An item that declares an effect your request does not name in <code>authority_effects</code>, such as running a command, is listed under <code>withheld</code> with its reason. A manifest or a read of it is refused with <code>item_withheld</code>. That is not a missing permission. Name the effect in the request when your step may have it.</p>
+<h2 id="two-kinds-of-credential">Two kinds of credential</h2>
+<p>The account page and your coding tool use different credentials, and the service keeps them apart.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Credential</th><th>Where you use it</th><th>What it can do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A browser session</td><td><code>/account</code>, after you sign in</td><td>Create, list and revoke client tokens, and start a checkout.</td></tr><tr><td>A client token</td><td>Your coding tool or a script</td><td>Search, read manifests, download bodies and read usage.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>A client token cannot manage credentials. That attempt is refused with <code>browser_session_required</code>, status 403, so a leaked token cannot make more tokens.</p>
+<p>The account page reads the token policy from <code>/api/v1/account/access</code>, in the <code>service_client_access_options/v1</code> record:</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>allowed_scopes</code></td><td>The scopes a new token may carry.</td></tr><tr><td><code>maximum_active_tokens</code></td><td>How many tokens you may hold at once. Past it, a new token is refused with <code>access_token_limit_reached</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>maximum_lifetime_seconds</code></td><td>The longest lifetime you may ask for. Longer is refused with <code>access_lifetime_exceeded</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>default_lifetime_seconds</code></td><td>The lifetime you get when you ask for none.</td></tr><tr><td><code>maximum_token_records</code></td><td>How many token records the account keeps. When they are all used, a new token is refused with <code>access_token_history_limit_reached</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
+<p>Three facts about a token secret:</p>
+<ul><li>It is shown once. The service stores only a digest, so nobody, your operator included, can show it to you again.</li><li>It expires. The account page shows a <code>state</code> beside each token, and a token whose state is <code>expired</code> or <code>revoked</code> is refused on its next request.</li><li>Revoking takes effect on the next request. It does not recall material that was already downloaded.</li></ul>
+<p><a href="/setup">Get set up</a> shows how to create a token and where to keep it.</p>
+<h2 id="the-plan">The plan</h2>
+<p>One plan, Baltor Pro, at $29 a month. Search is free. One downloaded item is the measured unit, and there is no overage billing. <a href="/docs/usage-and-what-you-pay-for">Usage and what you pay for</a> lists what is measured and what is not. A new customer starts at <a href="/get-started">Get started</a>.</p>
+<p>A signed-in account subscribes, or opens the payment portal, from its account page when checkout is available. The page reads what the service offers your account:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $BALTOR_SERVICE_TOKEN" \
+  https://app.baltor.ai/api/v1/billing/plans</code></pre>
+<p>The answer is a <code>billing_session_options/v1</code> record. When <code>checkout_available</code> or <code>portal_available</code> is false, <code>unavailable_reason</code> says why. <code>billing_sessions_not_installed</code> means the host has not switched payments on. <code>billing_scope_required</code> means the credential does not hold <code>billing:manage</code>. The other reasons describe the host, and only your operator can change them. The portal opens only after your first checkout, which creates your payment customer.</p>
+<h2 id="what-an-account-does-not-give-you">What an account does not give you</h2>
+<ul><li>It does not give you a model or pay for model calls. Baltor never runs a model. You bring your own model access, for example Ollama on your own machine, Ollama Cloud or a model server on your own network.</li><li>It does not let the service run anything on your machine.</li><li>It does not make downloaded material independently qualified. Every answer names its <code>qualification_basis</code>, and <code>host_attested</code> means that the host attests the approval.</li><li>It does not give each token its own tenant. The token policy says so directly: tokens share the selected tenant's grants and usage.</li></ul>
+<h2 id="related-pages">Related pages</h2>
+<ul><li><a href="/docs/what-baltor-is">What Baltor is</a></li><li><a href="/setup">Get set up</a></li><li><a href="/docs/usage-and-what-you-pay-for">Usage and what you pay for</a></li><li><a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li></ul>
